<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cyclic vomiting syndrome, provisional (R11.15)</content>. Good response to prophylaxis: one brief, aborted episode in three months at the target dose, against about two a month before treatment, which is more than a 50 percent reduction. Side effects are tolerable and the weight gain is 1.5 kg since treatment began. The diagnosis remains clinical and provisional; no new feature has appeared to suggest another cause. [S]<list><item>Continue amitriptyline 75 mg at bedtime for at least a year, then consider a slow taper if he stays well.</item><item>Continue the abortive plan and carry the emergency department letter.</item><item>Plan for travel: sleep the night before when possible, regular meals, and the abortive medicines in his hand luggage.</item><item>Review in three months, or sooner for more frequent or longer episodes.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Episode diary: number, length, and severity of episodes, prodrome, emergency visits, and missed work</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One episode in three months, about four hours, aborted at the prodrome; no emergency visits</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Rome IV criteria and red flags (vomiting between episodes, weight loss, nocturnal pain, neurological signs, onset after 50)</td><td>At diagnosis and with any change in pattern</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pattern unchanged and milder; no red flags</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count, electrolytes and glucose, liver panel, lipase, urinalysis, TSH, cortisol, celiac serology</td><td>At diagnosis between episodes; repeat only for an atypical episode</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at diagnosis; no atypical episode</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper endoscopy and small-bowel imaging (CT enterography)</td><td>Once at diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Gastric emptying study</td><td>Not routine; only between episodes when gastroparesis is suspected</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms of gastroparesis</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>ECG with QTc on amitriptyline and ondansetron (below 450 ms in men)</td><td>Baseline, at about 50 mg during titration, and at the target dose</td><td>Up to date</td><td>QTc 436 ms at 75 mg three months ago; repeat only with a dose change or a new QT-prolonging medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Amitriptyline tolerability: drowsiness, dry mouth, constipation, dizziness on standing, weight</td><td>Each visit on treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dry mouth persists; constipation and grogginess improved; weight 72.9 kg (up 1.5 kg since starting)</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and thoughts of self-harm on a tricyclic (PHQ-9)</td><td>Each visit on treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 3, with item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety screen (GAD-7)</td><td>At diagnosis, then every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>GAD-7 5, down from 8 at the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Abortive plan: timing and response to sumatriptan nasal spray and ondansetron, and dose limits</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Used once, at the prodrome, with relief in about four hours; within dose limits</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Individualized emergency department protocol letter</td><td>At diagnosis; update with any change in treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Letter carried; no change in treatment, so no update needed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Triggers: sleep, meals, travel, social stress, infections</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Overnight travel with short sleep triggered the one episode; a travel plan agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cannabis and other substance use, and hot-bathing behavior (cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome)</td><td>At diagnosis and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No cannabis use; recheck yearly</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comorbidities: migraine, orthostatic and autonomic symptoms, sleep disorders</td><td>At diagnosis and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No migraine, orthostatic symptoms, or sleep disorder</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Mitochondrial supplements (coenzyme Q10, riboflavin)</td><td>Conditional; consider with a partial response</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Good response to amitriptyline</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Alternative prophylaxis (topiramate or aprepitant)</td><td>Only if amitriptyline fails or is not tolerated</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Amitriptyline effective and tolerated</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relaxation training, meditation, or biofeedback; cognitive behavioral therapy offered</td><td>Ongoing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Breathing practice most evenings; he does not want cognitive behavioral therapy now, and the offer stays open</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and hydration</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>72.9 kg; well hydrated</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete the work-up between episodes (blood and urine tests, ECG, CT enterography, and upper endoscopy) before the six-week visit. Outcome by 1849-04-14: All tests normal; Rome IV criteria met; provisional diagnosis of cyclic vomiting syndrome at the consultation [S]</item><item>At least half as many episodes after three months at the target dose of prophylaxis. Outcome by 1849-09-08: About two episodes a month before treatment; one brief episode, aborted at the prodrome, in the three months before the six-month review [S]</item><item>Carry the abortive medicines and the emergency department letter, and use them at the prodrome. Outcome by 1849-06-09: The one episode since starting treatment was treated at the prodrome and settled in about ten hours without an emergency visit; letter carried [S]</item><item>QTc below 450 ms at the target amitriptyline dose. Outcome by 1849-06-09: QTc 436 ms on 75 mg [S]</item><item>Practice relaxation breathing most days and lower the GAD-7 score by the six-month review. Outcome by 1849-09-08: Daily breathing practice; GAD-7 from 8 to 5 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Guidelines on management of cyclic vomiting syndrome in adults by the American Neurogastroenterology and Motility Society and the Cyclic Vomiting Syndrome Association (2019); Anxiety Disorders in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in three months; at one year, discuss a slow taper of amitriptyline if he stays well. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
